+\noindent\textbf{Conclusion.}
+For this energy, the bifurcation patterns themselves are classical:
+a reflection-symmetric pitchfork at the centre produces an axial pair
+of saddles, and its rotation-symmetric counterpart produces a circle
+of saddles. What this paper determines, across the whole range of
+aspect ratios, is where these patterns occur
+($\cO<\lambda<\bO$ on the axis and $\bP<\lambda<\cP$ on the
+equator), where the stationary sets leave or enter through the boundary
+(the axial pair reaches the poles at $\bO$; the circle enters from
+the boundary equator at $\bP$), and that no further stationary
+components occur. The boundary events and the absence of other
+stationary components do not follow from general bifurcation theory.
+Consequently, an interior local minimizer exists exactly for
+$\cO<\lambda<\cP$, and in this range the centre is the only such
+minimizer. The full-family claims are conditional on C1--C6;
+constrained minimizers on the boundary have not been classified here.
+
