+\subsection{Why these windows: centre signs versus boundary signs}\label{subsec:why_windows}
+
+The windows in Table~\ref{tab:classification} can be understood by
+comparing a derivative near the centre with its trace at the boundary.
+On the oblate axis, let
+$g(z,\lambda)=\partial_z\mathcal E_\lambda(0,z)$,
+$a_c(\lambda)=g_z(0,\lambda)$, and
+$B_o(\lambda)=g(1^-,\lambda)$.
+Because $g(0,\lambda)=0$, the sign of $g$ for sufficiently small
+$z>0$ is the sign of $a_c$ whenever this curvature is nonzero.
+The signs supplied by the analytic end estimates and C1--C2 are
+\[
+\begin{array}{c|cc}
+\text{oblate range} & a_c(\lambda) & B_o(\lambda)\\ \hline
+0<\lambda<\cO & - & - \\
+\cO<\lambda<\bO & + & - \\
+\bO<\lambda<1 & + & +
+\end{array}
+\]
+Only the middle range has opposite signs. There $g$ is positive
+near the centre and negative at the north pole, so continuity forces
+at least one zero in $0<z<1$; reflection gives a pair on the axis.
+At the roots, C1 supplies positive transverse curvature and C2
+supplies $g_z<0$, making each an index-one saddle.
+
+For the prolate equator, write
+$H(\rho,0;\lambda)=\partial_\rho\mathcal E_\lambda(\rho,0)/\rho$,
+$h_c(\lambda)=H(0,0;\lambda)$, and
+$h_b(\lambda)=H(1^-,0;\lambda)$.
+The corresponding signs (from C3--C4 in the finite prolate range) are
+\[
+\begin{array}{c|cc}
+\text{prolate range} & h_c(\lambda) & h_b(\lambda)\\ \hline
+1<\lambda<\bP & + & + \\
+\bP<\lambda<\cP & + & - \\
+\cP<\lambda\le5 & - & -
+\end{array}
+\]
+In the middle range, $H$ is positive near the centre and negative at
+the equatorial boundary. Continuity gives an interior radial zero,
+and rotation turns that zero into a stationary circle. C3--C4
+supply the vertical exclusion, strict radial monotonicity and normal
+Hessian signs needed for the unique Morse--Bott saddle circle.
+The tail $\lambda\ge5$ is classified separately by C5--C6.
+
+The four window endpoints represent two different events.
+At $\cO$ and $\cP$, a \emph{central curvature} vanishes: the axial
+pair emerges from the centre as $\lambda$ increases past $\cO$,
+and the equatorial circle contracts into the centre as $\lambda$
+increases to $\cP$. In particular, Section~\ref{sec:finite_assembly}
+shows $z_*(\cO+)=0$.
+At $\bO$ and $\bP$, a \emph{boundary trace} vanishes:
+the axial pair reaches the poles at $\bO$ and the equatorial circle
+enters from the boundary at $\bP$. The same axial argument yields
+$z_*(\bO-)=1$.
+
+Opposite endpoint signs establish \emph{existence}, not uniqueness.
+They do not exclude further roots on the same line, nor stationary
+points away from the axis and equator. Those conclusions use the
+transverse sign arguments of Section~\ref{sec:principles}, the
+monotonicity and root counts of Section~\ref{sec:finite_assembly},
+and the specified inputs C1--C6. No extra computational claim is
+introduced here.
+
+\paragraph{Corollary (interior local minimizers).}
+Under Assertions C1--C6, the classification in
+Table~\ref{tab:classification} implies that an interior local
+minimizer exists if and only if $\cO<\lambda<\cP$; in that range
+the centre is the unique interior local minimizer. At $\cO$ and
+$\cP$ the centre is a degenerate saddle, not an additional local
+minimum. The other interior stationary components are saddles.
+This statement does not concern minima constrained to the boundary.
+
 The interval $(\bO,\bP)$ contains the sphere. A circle is one connected critical manifold, not a finite collection of isolated roots. Its boundary arrival at $\bP$, and the axial pair's arrival at $\bO$, are excluded from the interior count. This classification does not select all constrained global minimizers on the closed body: a constrained boundary minimum can have nonzero normal derivative.
 
 The theorem is stated with a named, finite list of inputs so that its current proof status can be assessed. In a certified submission version, C1--C6 must be supplied as established computational propositions with an accessible, fixed reproduction package. Removing the assumption from the wording without establishing those propositions would not strengthen the result.
