+
+Two further uniqueness results illustrate how much the assumptions on the
+functional matter.  In the preprint \emph{Uniqueness of radial centers of
+parallel bodies}, O'Hara proves that, for a compact convex set
+$\widetilde\Omega\subset\mathbb R^m$ with piecewise $C^1$ boundary,
+the parallel body $\widetilde\Omega+\delta B^m$ has a unique radial
+centre of every order when
+$\delta\geq\varphi(m)\operatorname{diam}(\widetilde\Omega)$,
+for a positive constant $\varphi(m)$ depending only on the dimension
+\citep{ohara2011parallel}. The author notes that some content of this
+preprint was later incorporated into his study of nearly spherical bodies
+\citep{ohara2022nearly}.
+In a different setting, Cabr\'e and Chanillo prove that a positive,
+semistable classical solution of $-\Delta u=f(u)$ with $u=0$ on the
+boundary has exactly one interior critical point, a nondegenerate maximum,
+when the planar domain is smooth, bounded, and has strictly positive
+boundary curvature and $f$ is smooth and nonnegative. They also obtain
+an analogue for smooth, bounded, strictly convex domains of revolution in
+higher dimensions under corresponding solution assumptions
+\citep{cabreChanillo1998stable}. Their result concerns the critical points
+of a specific elliptic boundary-value problem, not arbitrary functions on
+convex domains, and does not imply uniqueness for the present angular energy.
+
+The \emph{local pattern} by which noncentral stationary sets are born has
+its own prior theory. Golubitsky and Schaeffer develop the classification of
+bifurcations with reflection ($\mathbb Z_2$) symmetry, including the
+pitchfork pattern \citep{golubitskySchaeffer1985}. Golubitsky, Stewart,
+and Schaeffer develop steady-state bifurcation in the presence of continuous
+and discrete symmetries \citep{golubitskyStewartSchaeffer1988}.
+On a reflection-symmetric axis, the appearance of a pair of solutions
+related by $z\mapsto-z$ is the familiar pitchfork-type pattern. In an
+$O(2)$-invariant transverse plane, any one nonzero off-centre critical
+point generates a whole circular group orbit of critical points under
+rotation. Such local configurations are therefore natural from the
+viewpoint of equivariant bifurcation theory; neither the existence of
+the corresponding branch for this particular functional nor the precise
+parameter window or exclusion of other branches is established merely by
+citing that general theory.
+
+The distinction between interior critical points and boundary events also
+has a broader topological setting. Borodzik, N\'emethi and Ranicki develop
+Morse theory on manifolds with boundary and, under additional topological
+hypotheses, move an interior critical point to the boundary where it splits
+into boundary critical points \citep{borodzikNemethiRanicki2016}.
+Their notion of a boundary critical point is not the present paper's
+condition $g_\partial=0$ for the \emph{entire} interior gradient trace.
+In particular, that theory alone does not determine either the pole-arrival
+ratio $\bO$ or the equatorial boundary-entry ratio $\bP$ here.
 
